course: dictionary
lesson: 2887
title: Dormant: asleep, but not gone
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Dormant means not active for a time, but able to wake up again. Think of a sleeping dog: it is resting, not dead, and it can jump up at any moment.
explain: «Some snails go dormant (shut up still, as if in a deep sleep) for long periods in dry weather, in rare cases even years, waiting for damp conditions.» The brackets tell you what dormant means here: still, as if in a deep sleep. The snail is not dead. It is waiting for damp weather.
explain: Look at the clues around the word. Long periods, dry weather and waiting for damp all point to resting until conditions are right. Swap in "resting" and the sentence still makes sense, so you have the meaning.
fact: Dormant is an adjective, a word that describes a noun.
fact: «Dormant» means not active or growing for a time, but able to become active again.
fact: A dormant volcano has not erupted for a long time, but it could erupt again.
fact: An extinct volcano will not erupt again, so it is not dormant.
fact: Seeds and bulbs stay dormant until spring, then start to grow.
exercise: «The volcano had been dormant for two hundred years.» What does dormant mean here? | Dead and gone forever | *Resting, not erupting for now | Covered in snow | Very hot and bubbling
exercise: Which thing is most likely dormant in winter? | *Tulip bulbs under the frozen soil | A boiling kettle | A bird singing on a branch | A busy bee on a flower
exercise: Ella says dormant means dead. Why is she wrong? | Dormant only describes people | Dormant means dead for ever | Dormant means very loud | *A dormant thing can wake up again
test: «A dormant volcano could erupt again one day, but an extinct one never will.» What is the difference? | *Dormant means it may wake up; extinct means it will not | They mean exactly the same thing | Dormant means it is cold; extinct means it is hot | Extinct means it is sleeping
test: Which is a good swap for dormant in «The seeds lie dormant in the soil»? | Painted | *Waiting, not growing yet | Dancing | Broken
exam: Which sentence uses dormant correctly? | *The bears stayed dormant in their dens all winter, waking in spring | The bears ran dormant across the field | Dormant, the sun was shining bright on the sea | The dormant children laughed loudly at the game
